					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drmagnus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(318209); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=318209&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607331599" alt="DrMagnus" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=318209)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drmagnus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(318209); return false;" >DrMagnus</a></span> <span class="odate time_1500383094 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Jul 2017 13:04</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3337984">
									

<p>To be <strong>completely</strong> pedantic, the sword shown isn't a Viking Era sword at all. The crossguard is too wide, and the brazil nut pomel wasn't in use during that period.</p>
<p>It's definitely a Carolingian or pre-knightly arming sword. I preserved it from the original article.</p>
<p>A &quot;true&quot; viking sword, is most typified by the very famous &quot;ulfbhert&quot; swords (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulfberht_swords">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulfberht_swords</a>).</p>
<p>Basically no crossguard, the knurled &quot;knuckle&quot; pomels, and double edged with no taper would typify a viking age sword.</p>

							</div>

								
					
